WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:13.000
Now that you have this foundational understanding of how Python works, we're going to learn how to structure our code into modules and work with different libraries, we talked a little bit about the main method early on in the class.

00:13.000 --> 00:25.000
Once you start writing your more comprehensive Python programs, if you're not using a library that runs your code for you, you want to have a main method.

00:25.000 --> 00:33.000
And the purpose of that main method is that it will only run when your code is executed as a standalone program.

00:33.000 --> 00:44.000
And because of how Python's import system works, if somebody else were to import your Python code, it is executed on import.

00:44.000 --> 00:50.000
So let's say we had a Python file like this called namelib.

00:50.000 --> 00:54.000
Go ahead and copy this.

00:54.000 --> 01:04.000
And let's create a new file and save this as main underscore lib.py.

01:05.000 --> 01:14.000
If we run this code, it looks exactly like we expected to, the length is for an uppercase version is meaner.

01:14.000 --> 01:28.000
But we want to write reusable code, because we went through all this trouble of writing this very, very useful library that has some methods in it, like getting the name for a length and the uppercase of that name.

01:28.000 --> 01:33.000
So how do we allow other programmers to use this?

01:33.000 --> 01:39.000
Let's say somebody else wrote a program called other program.

01:39.000 --> 01:51.000
And let's copy this and save it as other program.py.

01:51.000 --> 01:58.000
We're going to import as sorry here we call this main lib.

01:58.000 --> 02:04.000
So let me just update this example.

02:04.000 --> 02:10.000
If somebody wants to open a pull request, now's your time to shine.

02:10.000 --> 02:15.000
So let's go ahead and run this.

02:15.000 --> 02:23.000
We'll see, I don't have Nina anywhere in here, but this is printing out the uppercase version is Nina.

02:23.000 --> 02:28.000
What is going on?

02:28.000 --> 02:36.000
I'm trying to use these methods from this main lib module.

02:36.000 --> 02:46.000
What's happening is when it is imported, all of the code in here is run, including this stuff down here.

02:46.000 --> 02:50.000
And that was just stuff that I needed for myself.

02:50.000 --> 02:55.000
If somebody is using this module as a library, they probably don't care about it.

02:55.000 --> 03:04.000
So that's why you want to wrap this code in a main method.

03:04.000 --> 03:25.000
So in order to do that, you ask if the value of dundername, which is one of those magic methods, magic variables, is equal to underscore the string underscore underscore mean, then run this code.

03:25.000 --> 03:33.000
Now if I run main lib, I see the length is for and the uppercase version is Nina.

03:33.000 --> 03:40.000
If I go back to my other program and I run it again, it's not printing out anything about Nina anymore.

03:40.000 --> 03:47.000
That's because when I run this from the command line, I am hitting this code in the main method.

03:47.000 --> 03:55.000
But when I import this program into another file that code in the main method is not being run.

03:55.000 --> 04:00.000
So that's an important distinction that will help you write reusable code.

